  /* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Tool marks: page ink at rest, brand colour on hover.

     Hovering the whole row rather than the 20px mark — the row is the thing
     that reads as one item, and a 20px hover target is a poor one.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  /*
    Sized in em, not px: the mark sits on the baseline of its own label, so it
    should track that text rather than drift away from it at the responsive type
    steps. 1.75em is ~28px at the default step — up from a flat 20px, which read
    as an afterthought next to a 600-weight display face.

    The glyph is the mask and the colour is painted underneath it, so the SVG's
    own fill never renders and one file covers both themes.
  */
  .tool__mark {
    display: inline-block;
    flex: none;
    position: relative;
    width: 1.75em;
    height: 1.75em;
    background-color: var(--ink);
    -webkit-mask: var(--mark) center / contain no-repeat;
    mask: var(--mark) center / contain no-repeat;
  }

  /*
    n8n's glyph is a node graph — genuinely 1.9:1, where the other three are
    square. Held in a square box `contain` letterboxes it to ~28x15 and it reads
    as the runt of the row. Matching the others on HEIGHT and taking its natural
    width is what makes it look the same size, which is what the eye actually
    compares. ~53px still fits the 9rem name column beside the word "n8n".
  */
  .tool__mark--n8n {
    width: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 152 / 80;
  }

  /*
    The brand colour rides on ::after and cross-fades its opacity, rather than
    swapping background-color on hover. Two reasons: background-image is not
    animatable, so Gemini's gradient would snap in while the flat marks faded,
    and this way every mark uses one transition and they all feel the same.
    The parent's mask clips the pseudo-element, so it takes the glyph's shape.
  */
  .tool__mark::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity var(--dur-mid) var(--ease-out);
  }

  .tool__mark--claude::after {
    background: #d97757;
  }

  /*
    OpenAI's purple is the one brand colour here that is only legible on light
    paper: #412991 measures 10.15:1 on --paper but 1.76:1 on dark --paper, so in
    dark mode the mark did not turn purple, it vanished. Every other mark lands
    at 5-6:1 in dark (claude 6.08, gemini 5.12-6.09), which is what made this one
    read as a bug rather than a look.

    So it gets a lighter tint of the same hue in dark mode — the same thing the
    site already does for --accent (#d97757 -> #e08a69 in tokens.css). #9b84de is
    6.10:1 on dark paper, i.e. it now sits exactly where Claude's mark sits.
    Fidelity is kept where it can be seen and traded only where it could not.
  */
  .tool__mark--chatgpt::after {
    background: #412991;
  }

  :root[data-theme='dark'] .tool__mark--chatgpt::after {
    background: #9b84de;
  }

  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme='light']) .tool__mark--chatgpt::after {
      background: #9b84de;
    }
  }

  /* Gemini's mark is a gradient in Google's own branding, not a flat colour —
     masking is what makes rendering it as one practical. */
  .tool__mark--gemini::after {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #4796e3 0%, #9177c7 50%, #d96570 100%);
  }

  /* n8n's pink needs no per-theme tint: 5.19:1 on dark paper (level with
     Gemini's 5.12) and 3.45:1 on light (better than Claude's 2.95). */
  .tool__mark--n8n::after {
    background: #ea4b71;
  }

  /* Gated on a real pointer: on touch, :hover sticks after a tap and would
     leave one mark stuck colourful with no way to clear it. */
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .tool:hover .tool__mark::after {
      opacity: 1;
